{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01SW5g7AcKXGTup8w2fGQK3p","type":"tool_result","content":"## Implementation plan\n22\t\n23\t### Summary\n24\t\n25\tAdd a starter deterministic scanner, `scanners/missing-tests`, under `internal/roles/` (embedded for `ard init`, like `scanners/cve`). It is Go only for now. For each Go module in the repository it:\n26\t\n27\t1. runs the module's tests once with coverage across the whole module (`go test -coverpkg=./... -coverprofile`);\n28\t2. statically reads which exported functions a test names;\n29\t3. reports one finding per untested function, grouped per package.\n30\t\n31\tThe role directory holds three files:\n32\t\n33\t- `scan`: a POSIX `sh` driver that follows the cve contract (`internal/roles/scanners/cve/scan`).\n34\t- `missingtests.go`: a stdlib-only Go helper with `//go:build ignore`. `scan` compiles it into its temp dir on each run.\n35\t- `Dockerfile`: the pinned Go image, on the same digest as cve's.\n36\t\n37\tNo controller code changes. The scanner pipeline from #23 (reconcile, backpressure, diff, group, file, autoclose) already does everything the issue asks for.\n38\t\n39\t### Measured on ard (origin/main, 2026-11-23, macOS, go1.27.4)\n40\t\n41\t`go test -count=1 -covermode=set -coverpkg=./... -coverprofile=c.out ./...` took 45 s and covered 1,278 functions:\n42\t\n43\t- 26 functions have 0% coverage: 17 exported and 9 unexported. Examples are `closeout.NewCommitter`, `scanner.ParseMarkers` and `servecmd.Serve`.\n44\t- A rough name check found 33 of 553 exported functions and methods that no `_test.go` file names. Examples are `config.Role.ReadsUntrusted`, `gitx.Git.ResetHard` and `costblock.Locate`. Several of the 33 are methods that the standard library calls implicitly, such as `Unwrap`, `GoString`, `LogValue` and `MarshalYAML`. That is why the implicit-method exemption below exists.\n45\t- `go tool cover -func` prints method names without their receiver (for example `Commit` and `Error`). It cannot give stable fingerprints, so a helper that parses the source is needed.\n46\t\n47\tExpect roughly 40 to 50 findings on ard. That is enough issues for Gate 3 (#53), which lowers `group_size` to 3.\n48\t\n49\t### Tech lead view\n50\t\n51\t**Architecture fit.**\n52\t\n53\t- This is a scanner role directory: an executable `scan` that prints the strict JSON-lines schema. The controller does all dedupe, grouping, filing and closing.\n54\t- Nothing in `internal/scanner`, `internal/executor` or `internal/config` changes. `config.inspectRole` already accepts the extra file (`missingtests.go` lands in `Role.Extra` and is mounted read-only).\n55\t- The executor builds the image with the role directory as its build context. The image tag is a hash of every file in the role directory (`imageTag`), so a change to the helper rebuilds the image.\n56\t- Like cve's Dockerfile, the image does not copy `scan` or the helper. The mounted files are the ones that run.\n57\t\n58\t**Dependencies.**\n59\t\n60\t- #23 is done.\n61\t- Not blocked by #41, #42 or #46, and it does not touch their code.\n62\t- #53 (Gate 3) needs this scanner enabled on ard. That is a human follow-up (see below).\n63\t- #51 (docs scanner, \"undocumented public APIs\") will need the same exported-symbol walk. Do not build a shared library now. Role directories are self-contained, and #51 can copy the parsing approach.\n64\t\n65\t**Security and invariants.**\n66\t\n67\t- *Invariants 1 and 2.* `scan` writes only to stdout, stderr and its own `mktemp` directory. It holds no GitHub token and no model key: `ExecEngine` passes neither.\n68\t- *Invariant 3.* The role ships in `internal/roles/`. ard's own `.ard/scanners/missing-tests/` and its `config.yaml` override are a human follow-up PR. `.ard/scanners/` is a protected path, and the worker's diff guard rejects writes there.\n69\t- *Invariant 6.* Titles, bodies and file names come from repository content, and the controller already escapes them.\n70\t- *Invariant 5 (dry-run).* There are no new side effects. Dry-run behaviour comes from the existing pipeline.\n71\t- *New exposure: this scanner executes repository code.* Unlike cve, which only loads packages, this scanner runs `origin/main`'s tests.\n72\t  - Under Docker this is bounded: read-only worktree, `--cap-drop ALL`, no tokens, egress only.\n73\t  - As a host subprocess (a role directory with no Dockerfile) the tests run as the controller's uid. They can read what `local.HostReach()` lists, including the App private key file.\n74\t  - `config.Role.ReadsUntrusted()` is false for deterministic scanners, so today no host-mode warning fires.\n75\t  - ard's own pod deployment already runs the worker's `make test` this way on branch code, which is worse. The risk is therefore not new, but it is new for scanners.\n76\t  - The starter ships a Dockerfile. Whether to also warn or refuse in host mode is open question 5.\n77\t- *Gaming the scanner.*\n78\t  - The opt-outs are human-owned files in the protected role directory, not in-code directives. A worker therefore cannot \"fix\" an issue by annotating the code (open question 7).\n79\t  - A worker that deletes the function or writes an empty test is caught by eng-review (\"missing tests, correctness\"), not by the scanner.\n80\t\n81\t**Operational risk.**\n82\t\n83\t- *Red `main`.* A failing `go test` fails the run. Nothing is filed or closed, and the job is recorded as failed. It retries on the next 24 h run. This matches cve's fail-closed rule. Falling back to static-only checking would flap autoclose (open question 4).\n84\t- *Flaky coverage.* A function reached only on a timing path could flip between covered and uncovered: it closes as fixed, then files again as a regression. The 0%-per-function threshold makes this rare. The `ignore` file is the remedy.\n85\t- *Read-only worktree.* A test that writes into its package directory fails under the scanner even though it passes under `make test`. The fix is to make that test use `t.TempDir()`, or to add `no-coverage`.\n86\t- *Volume.* The first run on a large repository could report thousands of findings. Filing is bounded by `max_open` and `global_max_open`, most severe first, and the rest stay unrecorded until the next run. Over 10,000 findings, the run fails closed (the pipeline's limit). The helper fails first with a clearer message.\n87\t- *Cost.* One full test run with coverage per module per day, in a scanner-class slot (lowest priority), within the 30 m default timeout.\n88\t- *Toolchain.* A module that needs a newer Go than the image downloads it, checksum-verified, from `proxy.golang.org`. This needs egress and an exec-capable `TMPDIR`. If either is missing, the run fails closed and says why, as in cve.\n89\t\n90\t**PR split.** One PR, roughly 600 lines of code plus fixtures, all in `internal/roles`, `internal/initcmd` tests and docs. If the reviewer prefers two, split it into:\n91\t\n92\t- PR 1: the helper, its fixtures and tests.\n93\t- PR 2: `scan`, the Dockerfile, the starter config, the e2e and Docker tests, and the docs.\n94\t\n95\t**Who builds it.** It suits ard's own engineer worker once the open questions are answered. It is self-contained and Go-plus-shell, with a strong existing pattern in cve. The `CLAUDE.md` Layout line is a protected path, so merging needs a human approval anyway.\n96\t\n97\t### Finding definition (the helper's rules)\n98\t\n99\t**Scope: which functions count.**\n100\t\n101\t- Functions are drawn from every package that `go list -json ./...` reports in the module: its `GoFiles` only. Build constraints are applied for the scan host, which under Docker means `linux`.\n102\t- `testdata`, `vendor`, directories starting with `.` or `_`, and nested modules are excluded by `./...` itself.\n103\t- Every `*ast.FuncDecl` with a body is considered, except:\n104\t  - functions named `main`, `init` or `_`;\n105\t  - functions whose body has no statements;\n106\t  - functions in files for which `ast.IsGenerated` is true.\n107\t\n108\t**Symbol.** The symbol is `Func` for a function. For a method it is `Recv.Method`, where `Recv` is the receiver's base type name with `*` and type parameters stripped. So `func (l *List[T]) Len()` is `List.Len`.\n109\t\n110\t**Exported.** A symbol is exported when its name is exported and, for a method, its receiver's base type is exported too. A method on an unexported type is treated as unexported.\n111\t\n112\t**Covered.** A function is covered when at least one coverage block inside its declaration extent (`fd.Pos()` to `fd.End()`, compared by line and then column, as `go tool cover` does) has a count above 0 in any profile entry. With `-coverpkg`, the same block appears once per test binary, so the entries are merged with OR. A function with statements but no profile blocks counts as uncovered.\n113\t\n114\t**Named by a test.** Only `_test.go` files in the same module are read (`TestGoFiles` and `XTestGoFiles` of every package):\n115\t\n116\t- *Package-level function `F` in package `P`.* It is named when either:\n117\t  - `F` appears as an `*ast.Ident` in one of `P`'s internal test files; or\n118\t  - some test file in the module has a selector `X.F` where `X` is that file's import name for `P`. The import name is the alias, or else `P`'s package `Name` from `go list`.\n119\t- *Method `T.M`.* It is named when some test file in the module has a selector whose `Sel` is `M`, or when `M` appears as an identifier in `P`'s internal tests. The receiver type is not checked. This approximation can only miss findings; it never invents one.\n120\t- *Implicit methods.* These names count as named whenever they are covered, because the standard library calls them implicitly (see open question 3): `Error`, `Unwrap`, `Is`, `As`, `String`, `GoString`, `Format`, `MarshalJSON`, `UnmarshalJSON`, `MarshalText`, `UnmarshalText`, `MarshalYAML`, `UnmarshalYAML`, `MarshalBinary`, `UnmarshalBinary`, `LogValue` and `ServeHTTP`. They are a `var implicitMethods` set in the helper.\n121\t\n122\t**When it is a finding, and its severity.**\n123\t\n124\t| Exported | Covered | Named by a test | Finding | Severity |\n125\t| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n126\t| yes | no | either | yes, \"is never run by tests\" | `medium` |\n127\t| yes | yes | no (and not an implicit method) | yes, \"has no test that calls it\" | `low` |\n128\t| yes | yes | yes | no | |\n129\t| no | no | n/a | yes, \"is never run by tests\" | `low` |\n130\t| no | yes | n/a | no | |\n131\t\n132\tWith `no-coverage` present, only the static rule applies: an exported, non-implicit symbol that no test names is a `low` finding. Coverage is never guessed.\n133\t\n134\t**What \"fixed\" means, stated in every body.** For an exported symbol: a test in this module calls it by name, and the module's tests execute it. For an unexported one: the module's tests execute it. When that holds, the fingerprint disappears and the autoclose rules close the issue.\n135\t\n136\t**Fingerprint.** `missing-tests:go:<import path>:<symbol>`, for example `missing-tests:go:github.com/albertcbrown/ard/internal/closeout:Committer.Commit`.\n137\t\n138\t- It does not include the signal, so one function is one finding for its whole life (open question 8). It also does not include the file or the line, so moving the function within its package keeps it.\n139\t- If the fingerprint is over 512 bytes, use `missing-tests:go:sha256:<hex of \"<import path>:<symbol>\">`.\n140\t\n141\t**Group.** `go:<import path>`, one group per package, so `group_size` 10 gives \"10 per package\".\n142\t\n143\t**Title.** At most 200 characters, cut with `…`. It is `<pkg>: <symbol> is never run by tests` or `<pkg>: <symbol> has no test that calls it`. `<pkg>` is the package directory relative to the repository root, or the module path when the package is at the repository root.\n144\t\n145\t**Files.** `[\"<repo-relative path of the declaring file>\"]`.\n146\t\n147\t**Body.** Markdown, kept under 1,900 characters so the 2,000-character details cut never truncates it:\n148\t\n149\t```\n150\t`<symbol>` in `<file>:<line>` (package `<import path>`, <exported|unexported>).\n151\t\n152\t- Statements run by the module's tests: <k> of <n>   (or: coverage not measured, `no-coverage` is set)\n153\t- Named by a test: <yes|no>\n154\t\n155\tFixed when <the rule above>. Measured with `go test -coverpkg=./... ./...` in module `<module dir>` using <go version>.\n156\t```\n157\t\n158\t**Output.** One JSON object per line, with keys in schema order (`fingerprint`, `title`, `severity`, `group`, `files`, `body`), sorted by fingerprint, so the output is deterministic.\n159\t\n160\t### Role directory: `internal/roles/scanners/missing-tests/`\n161\t\n162\t| File | Mode | Purpose |\n163\t| --- | --- | --- |\n164\t| `scan` | 0755 | sh driver |\n165\t| `missingtests.go` | 0644 | `//go:build ignore` helper. `go vet ./...` and `go build ./...` skip it, and so does ard's own missing-tests scan. Explicit `go build file.go` still builds it (verified). |\n166\t| `Dockerfile` | 0644 | `ARG GO_IMAGE=golang:1.27.4-trixie@sha256:f18c3d73…` (the exact line from `scanners/cve/Dockerfile`), then `FROM ${GO_IMAGE}`, `ENV GOTOOLCHAIN=local`, `ENV TMPDIR=/var/tmp`. The header comment says \"keep in step with scanners/cve/Dockerfile and the root Dockerfile\", and that the image deliberately copies neither `scan` nor the helper. No extra tools are needed: the golang image has git. |\n167\t\n168\tOptional files are human-owned and absent from the starter. Both are documented in the `scan` header and the proposal:\n169\t\n170\t- `ignore`: one pattern per line; blank lines and `#` comments are skipped. Patterns are matched against the key `<import path>:<symbol>`:\n171\t  - `<import path>` ignores that whole package;\n172\t  - `<import path>/...` ignores that package and every package below it;\n173\t  - `<import path>:<glob>` ignores matching symbols, using `path.Match` on the symbol (for example `…/fake:Fake.*`).\n174\t\n175\t  A malformed line fails the run and names the line number. A pattern that matches nothing logs a warning to stderr.\n176\t- `no-coverage`: skips `go test`, so the scan is static only. It is for repositories whose tests need services or cannot run on a read-only tree.\n177\t\n178\t### `scan` (sh, structure mirrors cve's)\n179\t\n180\tThe header comment states the same contract as cve's:\n181\t\n182\t- the current directory is a read-only worktree;\n183\t- siblin","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"3f643df0-b01a-42ba-9425-0eba70017c83","uuid":"7f5d334d-7c15-4497-b9cd-c1f8935e6eb5","timestamp":"2026-11-23T16:18:57.305Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"## Implementation plan\n22\t\n23\t### Summary\n24\t\n25\tAdd a starter deterministic scanner, `scanners/missing-tests`, under `internal/roles/` (embedded for `ard init`, like `scanners/cve`). It is Go only for now. For each Go module in the repository it:\n26\t\n27\t1. runs the module's tests once with coverage across the whole module (`go test -coverpkg=./... -coverprofile`);\n28\t2. statically reads which exported functions a test names;\n29\t3. reports one finding per untested function, grouped per package.\n30\t\n31\tThe role directory holds three files:\n32\t\n33\t- `scan`: a POSIX `sh` driver that follows the cve contract (`internal/roles/scanners/cve/scan`).\n34\t- `missingtests.go`: a stdlib-only Go helper with `//go:build ignore`. `scan` compiles it into its temp dir on each run.\n35\t- `Dockerfile`: the pinned Go image, on the same digest as cve's.\n36\t\n37\tNo controller code changes. The scanner pipeline from #23 (reconcile, backpressure, diff, group, file, autoclose) already does everything the issue asks for.\n38\t\n39\t### Measured on ard (origin/main, 2026-11-23, macOS, go1.27.4)\n40\t\n41\t`go test -count=1 -covermode=set -coverpkg=./... -coverprofile=c.out ./...` took 45 s and covered 1,278 functions:\n42\t\n43\t- 26 functions have 0% coverage: 17 exported and 9 unexpor