Scope: which functions count.**\n100\t\n101\t- Functions are drawn from every package that `go list -json ./...` reports in the module: its `GoFiles` only. Build constraints are applied for the scan host, which under Docker means `linux`.\n102\t- `testdata`, `vendor`, directories starting with `.` or `_`, and nested modules are excluded by `./...` itself.\n103\t- Every `*ast.FuncDecl` with a body is considered, except:\n104\t  - functions named `main`, `init` or `_`;\n105\t  - functions whose body has no statements;\n106\t  - functions in files for which `ast.IsGenerated` is true.\n107\t\n108\t**Symbol.** The symbol is `Func` for a function. For a method it is `Recv.Method`, where `Recv` is the receiver's base type name with `*` and type parameters stripped. So `func (l *List[T]) Len()` is `List.Len`.\n109\t\n110\t**Exported.** A symbol is exported when its name is exported and, for a method, its receiver's base type is exported too. A method on an unexported type is treated as unexported.\n111\t\n112\t**Covered.** A function is covered when at least one coverage block inside its declaration extent (`fd.Pos()` to `fd.End()`, compared by line and then column, as `go tool cover` does) has a count above 0 in any profile entry. With `-coverpkg`, the same block appears once per test binary, so the entries are merged with OR. A function with statements but no profile blocks counts as uncovered.\n113\t\n114\t**Named by a test.** Only `_test.go` files in the same module are read (`TestGoFiles` and `XTestGoFiles` of every package):\n115\t\n116\t- *Package-level function `F` in package `P`.* It is named when either:\n117\t  - `F` appears as an `*ast.Ident` in one of `P`'s internal test files; or\n118\t  - some test file in the module has a selector `X.F` where `X` is that file's import name for `P`. The import name is the alias, or else `P`'s package `Name` from `go list`.\n119\t- *Method `T.M`.* It is named when some test file in the module has a selector whose `Sel` is `M`, or when `M` appears as an identifier in `P`'s internal tests. The receiver type is not checked. This approximation can only miss findings; it never invents one.\n120\t- *Implicit methods.* These names count as named whenever they are covered, because the standard library calls them implicitly (see open question 3): `Error`, `Unwrap`, `Is`, `As`, `String`, `GoString`, `Format`, `MarshalJSON`, `UnmarshalJSON`, `MarshalText`, `UnmarshalText`, `MarshalYAML`, `UnmarshalYAML`, `MarshalBinary`, `UnmarshalBinary`, `LogValue` and `ServeHTTP`. They are a `var implicitMethods` set in the helper.\n121\t\n122\t**When it is a finding, and its severity.**\n123\t\n124\t| Exported | Covered | Named by a test | Finding | Severity |\n125\t| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n126\t| yes | no | either | yes, \"is never run by tests\" | `medium` |\n127\t| yes | yes | no (and not an implicit method) | yes, \"has no test that calls it\" | `low` |\n128\t| yes | yes | yes | no | |\n129\t| no | no | n/a | yes, \"is never run by tests\" | `low` |\n130\t| no | yes | n/a | no | |\n131\t\n132\tWith `no-coverage` present, only the static rule applies: an exported, non-implicit symbol that no test names is a `low` finding. Coverage is never guessed.\n133\t\n134\t**What \"fixed\" means, stated in every body.** For an exported symbol: a test in this module calls it by name, and the module's tests execute it. For an unexported one: the module's tests execute it. When that holds, the fingerprint disappears and the autoclose rules close the issue.\n135\t\n136\t**Fingerprint.** `missing-tests:go:<import path>:<symbol>`, for example `missing-tests:go:github.com/albertcbrown/ard/internal/closeout:Committer.Commit`.\n137\t\n138\t- It does not include the signal, so one function is one finding for its whole life (open question 8). It also does not include the file or the line, so moving the function within its package keeps it.\n139\t- If the fingerprint is over 512 bytes, use `missing-tests:go:sha256:<hex of \"<import path>:<symbol>\">`.\n140\t\n141\t**Group.** `go:<import path>`, one group per package, so `group_size` 10 gives \"10 per package\".\n142\t\n143\t**Title.** At most 200 characters, cut with `…`. It is `<pkg>: <symbol> is never run by tests` or `<pkg>: <symbol> has no test that calls it`. `<pkg>` is the package directory relative to the repository root, or the module path when the package is at the repository root.\n144\t\n145\t**Files.** `[\"<repo-relative path of the declaring file>\"]`.\n146\t\n147\t**Body.** Markdown, kept under 1,900 characters so the 2,000-character details cut never truncates it:\n148\t\n149\t```\n150\t`<symbol>` in `<file>:<line>` (package `<import path>`, <exported|unexported>).\n151\t\n152\t- Statements run by the module's tests: <k> of <n>   (or: coverage not measured, `no-coverage` is set)\n153\t- Named by a test: <yes|no>\n154\t\n155\tFixed when <the rule above>. Measured with `go test -coverpkg=./... ./...` in module `<module dir>` using <go version>.\n156\t```\n157\t\n158\t**Output.** One JSON object per line, with keys in schema order (`fingerprint`, `title`, `severity`, `group`, `files`, `body`), sorted by fingerprint, so the output is deterministic.\n159\t\n160\t### Role directory: `internal/roles/scanners/missing-tests/`\n161\t\n162\t| File | Mode | Purpose |\n163\t| --- | --- | --- |\n164\t| `scan` | 0755 | sh driver |\n165\t| `missingtests.go` | 0644 | `//go:build ignore` helper. `go vet ./...` and `go build ./...` skip it, and so does ard's own missing-tests scan. Explicit `go build file.go` still builds it (verified). |\n166\t| `Dockerfile` | 0644 | `ARG GO_IMAGE=golang:1.27.4-trixie@sha256:f18c3d73…` (the exact line from `scanners/cve/Dockerfile`), then `FROM ${GO_IMAGE}`, `ENV GOTOOLCHAIN=local`, `ENV TMPDIR=/var/tmp`. The header comment says \"keep in step with scanners/cve/Dockerfile and the root Dockerfile\", and that the image deliberately copies neither `scan` nor the helper. No extra tools are needed: the golang image has git. |\n167\t\n168\tOptional files are human-owned and absent from the starter. Both are documented in the `scan` header and the proposal:\n169\t\n170\t- `ignore`: one pattern per line; blank lines and `#` comments are skipped. Patterns are matched against the key `<import path>:<symbol>`:\n171\t  - `<import path>` ignores that whole package;\n172\t  - `<import path>/...` ignores that package and every package below it;\n173\t  - `<import path>:<glob>` ignores matching symbols, using `path.Match` on the symbol (for example `…/fake:Fake.*`).\n174\t\n175\t  A malformed line fails the run and names the line number. A pattern that matches nothing logs a warning to stderr.\n176\t- `no-coverage`: skips `go test`, so the scan is static only. It is for repositories whose tests need services or cannot run on a read-only tree.\n177\t\n178\t### `scan` (sh, structure mirrors cve's)\n179\t\n180\tThe header comment states the same contract as cve's:\n181\t\n182\t- the current directory is a read-only worktree;\n183\t- siblin","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01SW5g7AcKXGTup8w2fGQK3p","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"rule"}}]}
